    "https://ferrumcollege.applytojob.com/apply/eqQDn5CQyj/Grounds-Enhancement-Technician": {
      "title": "Grounds Enhancement Technician",
      "description": "Grounds Enhancement Technician - Ferrum College - Career Page Skip To Job Description View All Jobs View Our Website Thank you for visiting our Careers page. We invite you to explore our current openings and apply for positions that align with your qualifications and interests. Grounds Enhancement Technician Ferrum, VA Full Time Physical Plant Entry Level Share Grounds Enhancement Technician Department: Physical Plant Employment Type: Full-Time, 12-Month Hourly Range: $12.77-$14.50 Reports To: Physical Plant Operations Manager Workweek: 40 hours per week Employment Status: Non-Exempt Who We Are? Ferrum College is committed to maintaining a beautiful, safe, and welcoming campus environment for students, faculty, staff, visitors, and the broader College community. The Grounds Department plays an important role in maintaining and enhancing the appearance of the Ferrum College campus. Through landscape installation, grounds maintenance, seasonal beautification projects, and support for campus events and operations, the Grounds team helps create an attractive and inviting environment for the entire College community. What We're Looking For Ferrum College is seeking a dependable, hardworking individual to help maintain and enhance the beauty, safety, and appearance of the College campus. This position focuses on creating and maintaining attractive campus landscapes, including flower beds, mulch beds, shrubs, trees, lawns, and other landscaped areas, while supporting the overall operations of the Grounds Department. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong work ethic, dependability, attention to detail, and the ability to work both independently and as part of a team. The position requires the ability to safely operate grounds-maintenance equipment and hand tools while working outdoors in a variety of weather conditions. As an essential employee, this position may be required to work beyond normal business hours and remain on campus during emergencies or critical events, including severe weather, snowstorms, power outages, and other situations requiring continuous campus operations. Key Responsibilities Grounds & Landscape Maintenance Maintain and enhance the appearance of the College campus through the installation and maintenance of flower beds, mulch beds, shrubs, ornamental plantings, trees, and landscaped areas. Develop and implement seasonal landscape enhancement projects that contribute to a welcoming and attractive campus environment. Perform mowing, trimming, edging, pruning, weeding, watering, fertilizing, mulching, and other grounds maintenance activities. Assist with tree, shrub, and landscape maintenance to ensure healthy and attractive campus grounds. Perform routine inspections of campus grounds and report safety hazards, equipment concerns, or maintenance needs. Perform general Groundskeeper duties as needed to support departmental operations. Equipment & Operations Operate and maintain a variety of motorized grounds equipment and hand tools, including mowers, tractors, trimmers, blowers, chainsaws, hedge trimmers, utility vehicles, and other equipment. Maintain equipment in safe operating condition and report repair needs promptly. Follow all College safety procedures and applicable OSHA regulations while performing assigned duties. Safely operate equipment and machinery in accordance with established procedures and safety requirements. Campus Events & Seasonal Operations Support campus events by assisting with grounds preparation, setup, cleanup, and restoration. Assist with snow and ice removal from roadways, sidewalks, parking lots, entrances, and other campus areas. Assist with seasonal grounds and landscape projects as needed. Perform other Grounds Department duties to support the safe and effective operation of the campus. Customer Service & Campus Support Provide courteous, professional, and responsive customer service to students, faculty, staff, visitors, and members of the external community. Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, students, faculty, and staff. Work cooperatively with members of the Grounds Department and other College departments. Participate in departmental training and professional development opportunities. Emergency & Essential Employee Responsibilities As an Essential Employee, this position is expected to: Report to work during College closures when directed. Remain on campus overnight, if necessary, during emergency situations such as severe weather events, snowstorms, extended power outages, or other campus emergencies. Assist with occasional emergency response and recovery efforts to maintain safe campus operations. Work overtime, evenings, weekends, holidays, or modified schedules as operational needs require. Who You Are? You are a dependable, hardworking, hands-on individual who takes pride in maintaining attractive and well-kept outdoor spaces. You enjoy working outdoors and understand the importance of maintaining a safe, clean, and welcoming campus environment. You are comfortable performing physically demanding work and operating a variety of landscaping equipment and tools. You can work independently while also contributing positively as part of a team. You demonstrate a strong work ethic, take direction well, communicate effectively, and are committed to providing courteous and professional service to the Ferrum College community. You are also willing to respond when operational needs require additional hours, including evenings, weekends, holidays, or emergency situations. Minimum Qualifications High School Diploma or GED required. One to three years of experience in grounds maintenance, landscaping, horticulture, or a related field preferred. Working knowledge of landscape maintenance techniques, plant materials, turf management, and grounds care. Ability to safely operate motorized equipment and hand tools. Valid driver's license with an acceptable driving record. Ability to work independently and as part of a team. Strong work ethic, dependability, and commitment to customer service. Preferred Qualifications Knowledge of landscaping, horticulture, and grounds maintenance practices. Ability to safely operate landscaping equipment and machinery. Ability to identify and address landscape maintenance needs. Ability to prioritize work and complete multiple assignments efficiently. What Makes This Opportunity Unique? The Grounds Enhancement Technician has a direct and visible impact on the Ferrum College campus every day. This position provides an opportunity to contribute to the appearance, safety, and overall experience of the College community through hands-on landscape maintenance, seasonal beautification, campus events, and grounds operations. The successful candidate will play an important role in keeping Ferrum's campus attractive, functional, and welcoming throughout the year. Physical & Working Environment This position requires the ability to work outdoors in varying weather conditions throughout the year. The successful candidate must be able to lift, carry, push, and pull up to 75 pounds and perform physically demanding work involving bending, kneeling, climbing, walking, and standing for extended periods. Work involves extensive outdoor activity in all weather conditions, including extreme heat, cold, rain, snow, and ice. The position may involve exposure to dust, noise, landscaping chemicals, and the operation of heavy equipment. Appropriate personal protective equipment must be worn as required. Additional Information This job description is intended to describe the general nature and level of work performed by employees assigned to this position. It is not intended to be an exhaustive list of all duties, responsibilities, or qualifications. Ferrum College reserves the right to modify or assign additional duties as operational needs require. Employees are expected to perform other duties as assigned. Ready to Join Our Team? If you are a dependable, hardworking, and dedicated grounds professional who takes pride in maintaining beautiful and safe outdoor spaces and enjoys being part of a team that makes a visible difference on campus, we encourage you to apply. Interested applicants should submit: Letter of Interest Resume Completed Employment Application Ferrum College is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Share Apply Apply for this position Required * First Name * Last Name * Email Address * Phone * Address Resume * We've received your resume. Click here to update it. Attach resume or Paste resume Attach resume as .pdf, .doc, .docx, .odt, .txt, or .rtf (limit 5MB) or Paste resume Paste your resume here or Attach resume file The following questions are entirely optional. To comply with government Equal Employment Opportunity and/or Affirmative Action reporting regulations, we are requesting (but NOT requiring) that you enter this personal data. This information will not be used in connection with any employment decisions, and will be used solely as permitted by state and federal law. Your voluntary cooperation would be appreciated. Learn more . Gender Decline to answer Female Male Race/Ethnicity Decline to answer Hispanic or Latino White, not Hispanic or Latino Black or African-American, not Hispanic or Latino Asian, not Hispanic or Latino Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander, not Hispanic or Latino American Indian or Alaskan Native, not Hispanic or Latino Two or More Races, not Hispanic or Latino MENA (Middle Eastern and North African) Invitation for Job Applicants to Self-Identify as a U.S. Veteran A “disabled veteran” is one of the following: a veteran of the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service who is entitled to compensation (or who but for the receipt of military retired pay would be entitled to compensation) under laws administered by the Secretary of Veterans Affairs; or a person who was discharged or released from active duty because of a service-connected disability. A “recently separated veteran” means any veteran during the three-year period beginning on the date of such veteran's discharge or release from active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval, or air service. An “active duty wartime or campaign badge veteran” means a veteran who served on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service during a war, or in a campaign or expedition for which a campaign badge has been authorized under the laws administered by the Department of Defense. An “Armed forces service medal veteran” means a veteran who, while serving on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service, participated in a United States military operation for which an Armed Forces service medal was awarded pursuant to Executive Order 12985. Veteran status I IDENTIFY AS ONE OR MORE OF THE CLASSIFICATIONS OF PROTECTED VETERAN LISTED ABOVE I AM NOT A PROTECTED VETERAN I DON’T WISH TO ANSWER Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Form CC-305 OMB Control Number 1250-0005 Expires 07/31/2029 Why are you being asked to complete this form? We are a federal contractor or subcontractor. The law requires us to provide equal employment opportunity to qualified\n                                            people with disabilities. We have a goal of having at least 7% of our workers as people with disabilities. The law says we\n                                            must measure our progress towards this goal. To do this, we must ask applicants and employees if they have a disability\n                                            or have ever had one. People can become disabled, so we need to ask this question at least every five years. Completing this form is voluntary, and we hope that you will choose to do so. Your answer is confidential. No one who\n                                            makes hiring decisions will see it. Your decision to complete the form and your answer will not harm you in any way. If you\n                                            want to learn more about the law or this form, visit the U.S. Department of Labor’s Office of Federal Contract Compliance\n                                            Programs (OFCCP) website at www.dol.gov/ofccp . How do you know if you have a disability? A disability is a condition that substantially limits one or more of your “major life activities.” If you have or have ever had\n                                            such a condition, you are a person with a disability. Disabilities include, but are not limited to: Alcohol or other substance use\n                                                disorder (not currently using\n                                                drugs illegally) Autoimmune disorder, for\n                                                example, lupus, fibromyalgia,\n                                                rheumatoid arthritis, HIV/AIDS Blind or low vision Cancer (past or present) Cardiovascular or heart\n                                                disease Celiac disease Cerebral palsy Deaf or serious difficulty\n                                                hearing Diabetes Disfigurement, for example,\n                                                disfigurement caused by burns,\n                                                wounds, accidents, or congenital\n                                                disorders Epilepsy or other seizure disorder Gastrointestinal disorders, for example,\n                                                Crohn's Disease, irritable bowel\n                                                syndrome Intellectual or developmental disability Mental health conditions, for example,\n                                                depression, bipolar disorder, anxiety\n                                                disorder, schizophrenia, PTSD Missing limbs or partially missing limbs Mobility impairment, benefiting from the\n                                                use of a wheelchair, scooter, walker,\n                                                leg brace(s) and/or other supports Nervous system condition, for example,\n                                                migraine headaches, Parkinson’s\n                                                disease, multiple sclerosis (MS) Neurodivergence, for example,\n                                                attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder\n                                                (ADHD), autism spectrum disorder,\n                                                dyslexia, dyspraxia, other learning\n                                                disabilities Partial or complete paralysis (any\n                                                cause) Pulmonary or respiratory conditions, for\n                                                example, tuberculosis, asthma,\n                                                emphysema Short stature (dwarfism) Traumatic brain injury Please check one of the boxes below: YES, I HAVE A DISABILITY, OR HAVE HAD ONE IN THE PAST NO, I DO NOT HAVE A DISABILITY AND HAVE NOT HAD ONE IN THE PAST I DO NOT WANT TO ANSWER PUBLIC BURDEN STATEMENT: According to the Paperwork Reduction Act of 1995 no persons are required to respond to a collection of information unless such collection displays a valid OMB control number. This survey should take about 5 minutes to complete. You must enter your name and date Name Date Human Check * Submit Application This website uses cookies and other analytics technologies. By selecting \"Allow\", you consent to the recording, use and sharing of your website activity by this website and its service providers. Allow Reject All Powered by"
    },
    "https://ferrumcollege.applytojob.com/apply/u0xGgLRraw/PartTime-Police-Officer": {
      "title": "Part-Time Police Officer",
      "description": "Part-Time Police Officer - Ferrum College - Career Page Skip To Job Description View All Jobs View Our Website Thank you for visiting our Careers page. We invite you to explore our current openings and apply for positions that align with your qualifications and interests. Part-Time Police Officer Ferrum, VA Part Time Law Enforcement Entry Level Share Police Officer Department: Campus Police Employment Type: Part Time/Seasonal Number of Positions Available : 4 Hourly Range: $18.00-$19.23 Reports To: Chief of Police Who We Are? Ferrum College Campus Police is committed to maintaining a safe, secure, and welcoming campus environment for students, faculty, staff, visitors, and the broader College community. The Campus Police Department provides law enforcement, emergency response, crime prevention, safety, and security services throughout the Ferrum College campus. Our officers work collaboratively with the campus community to promote safety, preserve peace, and support the College's mission. What We're Looking For Ferrum College is seeking a professional, dependable, and community-focused Police Officer to join the Campus Police Department. This is a part time, seasonal, non-supervisory position responsible for performing general police duties necessary to maintain the safety, security, and good order of the College community. The Police Officer works an assigned shift under the general guidance of the Chief of Police. The successful candidate will demonstrate sound judgment, attention to detail, excellent verbal and communication skills, and the ability to work independently and without direct supervision. The Police Officer will interact with students, parents, College officials, fellow employees, and members of the public while maintaining a professional law enforcement presence throughout the campus. Key Responsibilities Identify criminal offenders and criminal activity and, when appropriate, apprehend offenders and participate in subsequent court proceedings. Reduce opportunities for criminal activity through preventive patrol and other appropriate measures. Respond to individuals who are in danger of physical harm and provide emergency services. Promote and preserve peace throughout the campus community. Facilitate the safe movement of vehicular and pedestrian traffic. Identify potentially serious law enforcement or governmental concerns and report them through appropriate channels. Enforce institutional rules, regulations, and laws regarding student conduct as defined in the Student Handbook. Enforce parking regulations, including violations involving blocking or impeding traffic. Issue traffic tickets, summonses, and arrest warrants as authorized. Enforce institutional regulations related to vehicle identification and parking permits. Enforce applicable criminal and traffic laws of the Commonwealth of Virginia within the officer's jurisdiction of authority. Maintain a professional law enforcement presence at all times and respond consistently with law enforcement procedures. Pursue additional police training and professional development. File patrol reports at the conclusion of each shift. Maintain knowledge of all security systems for the Ferrum College campus, including security cameras and alarm systems, as well as the operational procedures for each. Work as a team member with the Student Affairs Office in the development and implementation of processes necessary to accomplish the goals and philosophy of the Student Affairs Office and the College. Assume special responsibilities or assignments as directed by the Chief of Police and/or designee, the Vice President of Operations, or the President. Engage the campus community in crime and safety prevention. Conduct full investigations on cases. Conduct foot patrols in and around academic buildings to identify and address security and safety issues. Conduct foot patrols in and around occupied and unoccupied residence halls to identify and address security and safety issues. The position may also oversee work-study students and provide assistance as requested. Who You Are? You are a professional law enforcement officer who understands the unique responsibilities of serving a college community. You are committed to maintaining a safe campus environment, preventing crime, preserving peace, and building positive working relationships with students and members of the Ferrum College community. You exercise good judgment, communicate effectively, pay close attention to detail, and are able to work independently. You are capable of responding appropriately to a variety of situations while maintaining professionalism and consistency in the performance of your duties. You are committed to maintaining your professional knowledge and skills through ongoing police training and development. Minimum Qualifications Certified in the Commonwealth of Virginia as a Police Officer. High school diploma or equivalent. Responsible work experience. Knowledge of community policing techniques. General knowledge of the rules of evidence and laws governing search and seizure. Ability to deal effectively with young adults. Ability to analyze evidence and conduct appropriate questioning and interviews. Ability to organize and prepare clear and concise oral and written reports. Skilled in the use of firearms and police equipment. Possession of the physical ability and endurance necessary to perform law enforcement duties. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with students, parents, school officials, fellow employees, and the public. Successful completion of required criminal history and credit checks. Preferred Qualifications Certified Community Police Specialist. DCJS Certified Basic Instructor. Ready to Join Our Team? Interested applicants should submit: Letter of Interest Resume Completed Employment Application Ferrum College is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Share Apply Apply for this position Required * First Name * Last Name * Email Address * Phone * Address Resume * We've received your resume. Click here to update it. Attach resume or Paste resume Attach resume as .pdf, .doc, .docx, .odt, .txt, or .rtf (limit 5MB) or Paste resume Paste your resume here or Attach resume file The following questions are entirely optional. To comply with government Equal Employment Opportunity and/or Affirmative Action reporting regulations, we are requesting (but NOT requiring) that you enter this personal data. This information will not be used in connection with any employment decisions, and will be used solely as permitted by state and federal law. Your voluntary cooperation would be appreciated. Learn more . Gender Decline to answer Female Male Race/Ethnicity Decline to answer Hispanic or Latino White, not Hispanic or Latino Black or African-American, not Hispanic or Latino Asian, not Hispanic or Latino Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander, not Hispanic or Latino American Indian or Alaskan Native, not Hispanic or Latino Two or More Races, not Hispanic or Latino MENA (Middle Eastern and North African) Invitation for Job Applicants to Self-Identify as a U.S. Veteran A “disabled veteran” is one of the following: a veteran of the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service who is entitled to compensation (or who but for the receipt of military retired pay would be entitled to compensation) under laws administered by the Secretary of Veterans Affairs; or a person who was discharged or released from active duty because of a service-connected disability. A “recently separated veteran” means any veteran during the three-year period beginning on the date of such veteran's discharge or release from active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval, or air service. An “active duty wartime or campaign badge veteran” means a veteran who served on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service during a war, or in a campaign or expedition for which a campaign badge has been authorized under the laws administered by the Department of Defense. An “Armed forces service medal veteran” means a veteran who, while serving on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service, participated in a United States military operation for which an Armed Forces service medal was awarded pursuant to Executive Order 12985. Veteran status I IDENTIFY AS ONE OR MORE OF THE CLASSIFICATIONS OF PROTECTED VETERAN LISTED ABOVE I AM NOT A PROTECTED VETERAN I DON’T WISH TO ANSWER Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Form CC-305 OMB Control Number 1250-0005 Expires 07/31/2029 Why are you being asked to complete this form? We are a federal contractor or subcontractor. The law requires us to provide equal employment opportunity to qualified\n                                            people with disabilities. We have a goal of having at least 7% of our workers as people with disabilities. The law says we\n                                            must measure our progress towards this goal. To do this, we must ask applicants and employees if they have a disability\n                                            or have ever had one. People can become disabled, so we need to ask this question at least every five years. Completing this form is voluntary, and we hope that you will choose to do so. Your answer is confidential. No one who\n                                            makes hiring decisions will see it. Your decision to complete the form and your answer will not harm you in any way. If you\n                                            want to learn more about the law or this form, visit the U.S. Department of Labor’s Office of Federal Contract Compliance\n                                            Programs (OFCCP) website at www.dol.gov/ofccp . How do you know if you have a disability? A disability is a condition that substantially limits one or more of your “major life activities.” If you have or have ever had\n                                            such a condition, you are a person with a disability. Disabilities include, but are not limited to: Alcohol or other substance use\n                                                disorder (not currently using\n                                                drugs illegally) Autoimmune disorder, for\n                                                example, lupus, fibromyalgia,\n                                                rheumatoid arthritis, HIV/AIDS Blind or low vision Cancer (past or present) Cardiovascular or heart\n                                                disease Celiac disease Cerebral palsy Deaf or serious difficulty\n                                                hearing Diabetes Disfigurement, for example,\n                                                disfigurement caused by burns,\n                                                wounds, accidents, or congenital\n                                                disorders Epilepsy or other seizure disorder Gastrointestinal disorders, for example,\n                                                Crohn's Disease, irritable bowel\n                                                syndrome Intellectual or developmental disability Mental health conditions, for example,\n                                                depression, bipolar disorder, anxiety\n                                                disorder, schizophrenia, PTSD Missing limbs or partially missing limbs Mobility impairment, benefiting from the\n                                                use of a wheelchair, scooter, walker,\n                                                leg brace(s) and/or other supports Nervous system condition, for example,\n                                                migraine headaches, Parkinson’s\n                                                disease, multiple sclerosis (MS) Neurodivergence, for example,\n                                                attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder\n                                                (ADHD), autism spectrum disorder,\n                                                dyslexia, dyspraxia, other learning\n                                                disabilities Partial or complete paralysis (any\n                                                cause) Pulmonary or respiratory conditions, for\n                                                example, tuberculosis, asthma,\n                                                emphysema Short stature (dwarfism) Traumatic brain injury Please check one of the boxes below: YES, I HAVE A DISABILITY, OR HAVE HAD ONE IN THE PAST NO, I DO NOT HAVE A DISABILITY AND HAVE NOT HAD ONE IN THE PAST I DO NOT WANT TO ANSWER PUBLIC BURDEN STATEMENT: According to the Paperwork Reduction Act of 1995 no persons are required to respond to a collection of information unless such collection displays a valid OMB control number. This survey should take about 5 minutes to complete. You must enter your name and date Name Date Human Check * Submit Application This website uses cookies and other analytics technologies. By selecting \"Allow\", you consent to the recording, use and sharing of your website activity by this website and its service providers. Allow Reject All Powered by"
