    "https://ferrumcollege.applytojob.com/apply/ztDsIt4YmX/Police-Officer": {
      "title": "Police Officer",
      "description": "Police Officer - Ferrum College - Career Page Skip To Job Description View All Jobs View Our Website Thank you for visiting our Careers page. We invite you to explore our current openings and apply for positions that align with your qualifications and interests. Police Officer Ferrum, VA Full Time Law Enforcement Entry Level Share Police Officer Department: Campus Police Employment Type: Full-Time Number of Positions : 4 Salary: $38,000–$40,000 annually, commensurate with experience and qualifications Reports To: Chief of Police Who We Are? Ferrum College Campus Police is committed to maintaining a safe, secure, and welcoming campus environment for students, faculty, staff, visitors, and the broader College community. The Campus Police Department provides law enforcement, emergency response, crime prevention, safety education, and security services throughout the Ferrum College campus. Our officers work collaboratively with the campus community to promote safety, preserve peace, and support the College's mission. What We're Looking For Ferrum College is seeking a professional, dependable, and community-focused Police Officer to join the Campus Police Department. This is a non-supervisory position responsible for performing general law enforcement duties necessary to maintain the safety, security, and good order of the College community. The Police Officer works an assigned shift under the general guidance of the Chief of Police and designated supervisors. The successful candidate will demonstrate sound judgment, professionalism, strong communication skills, and the ability to work effectively with students, employees, parents, College officials, fellow officers, and members of the public. Key Responsibilities Identify criminal offenders and criminal activity and, when appropriate, apprehend offenders and participate in subsequent court proceedings. Conduct preventive patrols and other crime prevention activities to reduce opportunities for criminal activity. Respond to individuals who may be at risk of physical harm and provide appropriate emergency assistance. Promote and preserve peace throughout the campus community. Facilitate the safe movement of vehicular and pedestrian traffic. Identify potentially serious law enforcement or governmental concerns and report them through appropriate channels. Enforce institutional rules and regulations related to student conduct as established by the Student Handbook. Enforce parking regulations, including violations involving blocking or impeding traffic. Issue traffic tickets, summonses, warrants, and arrest warrants as authorized. Enforce institutional vehicle identification and parking permit requirements. Enforce applicable criminal and traffic laws of the Commonwealth of Virginia within the officer's jurisdiction of authority. Conduct investigations and prepare clear, concise, and accurate reports. Conduct foot patrols in and around academic buildings to identify and address safety and security concerns. Conduct foot patrols in occupied and unoccupied residence halls to identify and address safety and security concerns. Maintain knowledge of campus security systems, including security cameras and alarm systems, and follow established operating procedures. Maintain a professional law enforcement presence and respond consistently and appropriately to incidents. File required patrol reports at the conclusion of each shift. Engage the campus community in crime prevention, safety awareness, and other community policing initiatives. Pursue additional police training and professional development as required. Work collaboratively with the Student Affairs Office and other College departments to support campus safety and institutional objectives. Assume special responsibilities and assignments as directed by the Chief of Police or designee, Vice President of Operations, or President. Perform other duties as assigned. Who You Are? You are a professional law enforcement officer who understands the unique responsibilities of serving a college community. You are committed to community policing, crime prevention, safety, and maintaining positive relationships with students and members of the Ferrum community. You exercise sound judgment, remain calm under pressure, and communicate effectively with individuals from diverse backgrounds. You are physically capable of performing the essential duties of the position and committed to maintaining your professional knowledge and skills through ongoing training. Minimum Qualifications Current Virginia certification as a Police Officer. High school diploma or equivalent. Responsible work experience. Knowledge of community policing techniques. General knowledge of rules of evidence and laws governing search and seizure. Ability to work effectively with young adults. Ability to analyze evidence and conduct appropriate questioning and interviews. Ability to prepare clear and concise oral and written reports. Skilled in the use of firearms and police equipment. Physical ability and endurance necessary to perform law enforcement duties. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with students, parents, College officials, coworkers, and the public. Successful completion of required criminal history and credit checks. Preferred Qualifications Certified Community Police Specialist. DCJS Certified Basic Instructor. What We Offer At Ferrum College, you'll have the opportunity to contribute directly to the safety and well-being of a close-knit college community while working as part of a professional campus police team. We offer: Comprehensive benefits package in accordance with College policies. Professional development and continuing law enforcement training opportunities. A collaborative campus environment. The opportunity to engage directly with students and the College community through crime prevention, safety education, and community policing. The opportunity to support a safe and welcoming environment where students can learn, live, and thrive. Ready to Join Our Team? Interested applicants should submit: Letter of Interest Resume Completed Employment Application Applications will be accepted immediately and the position will remain open until filled. Employment is contingent upon the successful completion of required background and credit checks. Ferrum College is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Share Apply Apply for this position Required * First Name * Last Name * Email Address * Phone * Address * Resume * We've received your resume. Click here to update it. Attach resume or Paste resume Attach resume as .pdf, .doc, .docx, .odt, .txt, or .rtf (limit 5MB) or Paste resume Paste your resume here or Attach resume file Cover Letter Who referred you to this position? Enter their first and last name here. Do you have a valid driver's license? * No answer Yes No Are you willing to relocate? No answer Yes No What's your citizenship / employment eligibility? * No answer I am a U.S. Citizen/Permanent Resident Non-citizen allowed to work for any employer Non-citizen allowed to work for current employer Non-citizen seeking work authorization I am a Canadian Citizen/Permanent Resident Other What's your highest level of education completed? * No answer GED or Equivalent High School Some College College - Associates College - Bachelor of Arts College - Bachelor of Fine Arts College - Bachelor of Science College - Master of Arts College - Master of Science College - Master of Fine Arts College - Master of Business Administration College - Doctorate Medical Doctor Other College or University GPA Are you 18 years of age or older? * No answer Yes No LinkedIn Profile URL: Desired salary Earliest start date? Can you work weekends? No answer Yes No Can you work evenings? No answer Yes No Can you work overtime? No answer Yes No Have you ever been convicted of a felony? * No answer No Yes If “Yes”, you have been convicted of a felony, please explain the circumstances around the conviction: References: Please enter names and contact information: * Upload Cover Letter The following questions are entirely optional. To comply with government Equal Employment Opportunity and/or Affirmative Action reporting regulations, we are requesting (but NOT requiring) that you enter this personal data. This information will not be used in connection with any employment decisions, and will be used solely as permitted by state and federal law. Your voluntary cooperation would be appreciated. Learn more . Gender Decline to answer Female Male Race/Ethnicity Decline to answer Hispanic or Latino White, not Hispanic or Latino Black or African-American, not Hispanic or Latino Asian, not Hispanic or Latino Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander, not Hispanic or Latino American Indian or Alaskan Native, not Hispanic or Latino Two or More Races, not Hispanic or Latino MENA (Middle Eastern and North African) Invitation for Job Applicants to Self-Identify as a U.S. Veteran A “disabled veteran” is one of the following: a veteran of the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service who is entitled to compensation (or who but for the receipt of military retired pay would be entitled to compensation) under laws administered by the Secretary of Veterans Affairs; or a person who was discharged or released from active duty because of a service-connected disability. A “recently separated veteran” means any veteran during the three-year period beginning on the date of such veteran's discharge or release from active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval, or air service. An “active duty wartime or campaign badge veteran” means a veteran who served on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service during a war, or in a campaign or expedition for which a campaign badge has been authorized under the laws administered by the Department of Defense. An “Armed forces service medal veteran” means a veteran who, while serving on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service, participated in a United States military operation for which an Armed Forces service medal was awarded pursuant to Executive Order 12985. Veteran status I IDENTIFY AS ONE OR MORE OF THE CLASSIFICATIONS OF PROTECTED VETERAN LISTED ABOVE I AM NOT A PROTECTED VETERAN I DON’T WISH TO ANSWER Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Form CC-305 OMB Control Number 1250-0005 Expires 07/31/2029 Why are you being asked to complete this form? We are a federal contractor or subcontractor. The law requires us to provide equal employment opportunity to qualified\n                                            people with disabilities. We have a goal of having at least 7% of our workers as people with disabilities. The law says we\n                                            must measure our progress towards this goal. To do this, we must ask applicants and employees if they have a disability\n                                            or have ever had one. People can become disabled, so we need to ask this question at least every five years. Completing this form is voluntary, and we hope that you will choose to do so. Your answer is confidential. No one who\n                                            makes hiring decisions will see it. Your decision to complete the form and your answer will not harm you in any way. If you\n                                            want to learn more about the law or this form, visit the U.S. Department of Labor’s Office of Federal Contract Compliance\n                                            Programs (OFCCP) website at www.dol.gov/ofccp . How do you know if you have a disability? A disability is a condition that substantially limits one or more of your “major life activities.” If you have or have ever had\n                                            such a condition, you are a person with a disability. Disabilities include, but are not limited to: Alcohol or other substance use\n                                                disorder (not currently using\n                                                drugs illegally) Autoimmune disorder, for\n                                                example, lupus, fibromyalgia,\n                                                rheumatoid arthritis, HIV/AIDS Blind or low vision Cancer (past or present) Cardiovascular or heart\n                                                disease Celiac disease Cerebral palsy Deaf or serious difficulty\n                                                hearing Diabetes Disfigurement, for example,\n                                                disfigurement caused by burns,\n                                                wounds, accidents, or congenital\n                                                disorders Epilepsy or other seizure disorder Gastrointestinal disorders, for example,\n                                                Crohn's Disease, irritable bowel\n                                                syndrome Intellectual or developmental disability Mental health conditions, for example,\n                                                depression, bipolar disorder, anxiety\n                                                disorder, schizophrenia, PTSD Missing limbs or partially missing limbs Mobility impairment, benefiting from the\n                                                use of a wheelchair, scooter, walker,\n                                                leg brace(s) and/or other supports Nervous system condition, for example,\n                                                migraine headaches, Parkinson’s\n                                                disease, multiple sclerosis (MS) Neurodivergence, for example,\n                                                attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder\n                                                (ADHD), autism spectrum disorder,\n                                                dyslexia, dyspraxia, other learning\n                                                disabilities Partial or complete paralysis (any\n                                                cause) Pulmonary or respiratory conditions, for\n                                                example, tuberculosis, asthma,\n                                                emphysema Short stature (dwarfism) Traumatic brain injury Please check one of the boxes below: YES, I HAVE A DISABILITY, OR HAVE HAD ONE IN THE PAST NO, I DO NOT HAVE A DISABILITY AND HAVE NOT HAD ONE IN THE PAST I DO NOT WANT TO ANSWER PUBLIC BURDEN STATEMENT: According to the Paperwork Reduction Act of 1995 no persons are required to respond to a collection of information unless such collection displays a valid OMB control number. This survey should take about 5 minutes to complete. You must enter your name and date Name Date Human Check * Submit Application This website uses cookies and other analytics technologies. By selecting \"Allow\", you consent to the recording, use and sharing of your website activity by this website and its service providers. Allow Reject All Powered by"
    },
    "https://ferrumcollege.applytojob.com/apply/DNy0fvi0kq/Adjunct-Clinical-Faculty": {
      "title": "Adjunct Clinical Faculty",
      "description": "Adjunct Clinical Faculty - Ferrum College - Career Page Skip To Job Description View All Jobs View Our Website Thank you for visiting our Careers page. We invite you to explore our current openings and apply for positions that align with your qualifications and interests. Adjunct Clinical Faculty Ferrum, VA Part Time Nursing Experienced Share Adjunct Clinical Faculty Department: Nursing Employment Type: Part-Time Hourly: $37.00 Reports To: Director of Nursing Workweek: 12 hours per week Who We Are Ferrum College is committed to providing students with meaningful educational opportunities that support their academic and professional development. The Nursing Division prepares students for the nursing profession through clinical instruction, hands-on learning, and guidance from qualified nursing professionals. What We’re Looking For Ferrum College is seeking a qualified and dedicated Adjunct Clinical Faculty member to support the Nursing Division. This individual will be responsible for planning, teaching, supervising, and evaluating nursing students in designated clinical settings. The ideal candidate will demonstrate strong clinical knowledge, effective teaching abilities, and a commitment to helping students develop the skills necessary to provide safe, quality patient care. Key Responsibilities Clinical Instruction and Student Supervision Plan, teach, and evaluate students in designated clinical areas. Supervise nursing students participating in clinical activities. Review clinical objectives with students and ensure clinical instruction aligns with course requirements. Select patient assignments and learning opportunities that support course objectives. Provide supervision and guidance to students in applying the nursing process. Conduct pre- and post-clinical conferences as needed. Complete midterm and final student evaluations using the QSEN clinical evaluation process. Provide student support, remediation, and tutoring as needed. Collaboration and Coordination Coordinate with course faculty regarding clinical rotation plans, assigned clinical agencies, and student group assignments. Establish and maintain cooperative working relationships with staff and personnel at clinical agencies. Become familiar with the Nursing Program's philosophy, objectives, and policies. Maintain familiarity with the Ferrum College catalog and applicable College policies. Professional Development and Student Support Maintain current knowledge and proficiency in the assigned clinical specialty. Use a variety of teaching and learning strategies to encourage inquiry and promote student learning. Serve as a role model, resource, and source of guidance for nursing students. Maintain excellent and consistent service to students. Attend meetings and programs as designated by the Dean and/or College. Participate in professional organizations at the local, state, and/or national levels. Perform other duties as assigned. Who You Are You are a dedicated nursing professional who values student learning, patient safety, and quality clinical care. You bring strong clinical judgment, effective communication skills, and the ability to guide students in applying nursing principles in real-world settings. You work collaboratively with faculty and clinical agency personnel and are committed to maintaining professional standards while supporting the development of future nurses. Minimum Qualifications Education: Nursing degree from an accredited college or university at the Bachelor of Science in Nursing (BSN) or Master of Science in Nursing (MSN) level. RN Licensure: Current, unencumbered Registered Nurse (RN) license or multistate licensure privilege authorizing practice in Virginia. Clinical Practice Background: A minimum of two years of direct client care as a Registered Nurse. Clinical Specialty: Documentation demonstrating a background in the specialty area in which students will be supervised, with a focus on quality and safety. Additional BSN Requirements: Clinical faculty members with a BSN must hold an alternative credential to meet clinical faculty requirements. Acceptable credentials include: Current certification in the specialty area related to the clinical assignment. Two years of active nursing clinical practice. Current certification as a Certified Nurse Educator (CNE) or Certified Nurse Educator–Clinical (CNEcl). Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated ability to teach, supervise, and evaluate nursing students in clinical settings. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Ability to establish positive working relationships with clinical agency personnel and nursing faculty. Commitment to student support, professional development, and safe patient care. What Makes This Opportunity Unique? This part-time position offers the opportunity to make a meaningful contribution to nursing education by helping students connect classroom learning with clinical practice. Adjunct Clinical Faculty members play an important role in developing students' clinical judgment, professional confidence, and commitment to safe, quality patient care. What We Offer Ferrum College offers the opportunity to contribute to a collaborative educational community and support the development of future nursing professionals. This part-time role provides an opportunity to share professional knowledge and clinical expertise while helping prepare students for careers in nursing. Ready to Join Our Team? Interested candidates should submit the following materials for consideration: Letter of Interest Resume Completed Employment Application Ferrum College is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Share Apply Apply for this position Required * First Name * Last Name * Email Address * Phone * Address Resume * We've received your resume. Click here to update it. Attach resume or Paste resume Attach resume as .pdf, .doc, .docx, .odt, .txt, or .rtf (limit 5MB) or Paste resume Paste your resume here or Attach resume file The following questions are entirely optional. To comply with government Equal Employment Opportunity and/or Affirmative Action reporting regulations, we are requesting (but NOT requiring) that you enter this personal data. This information will not be used in connection with any employment decisions, and will be used solely as permitted by state and federal law. Your voluntary cooperation would be appreciated. Learn more . Gender Decline to answer Female Male Race/Ethnicity Decline to answer Hispanic or Latino White, not Hispanic or Latino Black or African-American, not Hispanic or Latino Asian, not Hispanic or Latino Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander, not Hispanic or Latino American Indian or Alaskan Native, not Hispanic or Latino Two or More Races, not Hispanic or Latino MENA (Middle Eastern and North African) Invitation for Job Applicants to Self-Identify as a U.S. Veteran A “disabled veteran” is one of the following: a veteran of the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service who is entitled to compensation (or who but for the receipt of military retired pay would be entitled to compensation) under laws administered by the Secretary of Veterans Affairs; or a person who was discharged or released from active duty because of a service-connected disability. A “recently separated veteran” means any veteran during the three-year period beginning on the date of such veteran's discharge or release from active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval, or air service. An “active duty wartime or campaign badge veteran” means a veteran who served on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service during a war, or in a campaign or expedition for which a campaign badge has been authorized under the laws administered by the Department of Defense. An “Armed forces service medal veteran” means a veteran who, while serving on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service, participated in a United States military operation for which an Armed Forces service medal was awarded pursuant to Executive Order 12985. Veteran status I IDENTIFY AS ONE OR MORE OF THE CLASSIFICATIONS OF PROTECTED VETERAN LISTED ABOVE I AM NOT A PROTECTED VETERAN I DON’T WISH TO ANSWER Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Form CC-305 OMB Control Number 1250-0005 Expires 07/31/2029 Why are you being asked to complete this form? We are a federal contractor or subcontractor. The law requires us to provide equal employment opportunity to qualified\n                                            people with disabilities. We have a goal of having at least 7% of our workers as people with disabilities. The law says we\n                                            must measure our progress towards this goal. To do this, we must ask applicants and employees if they have a disability\n                                            or have ever had one. People can become disabled, so we need to ask this question at least every five years. Completing this form is voluntary, and we hope that you will choose to do so. Your answer is confidential. No one who\n                                            makes hiring decisions will see it. Your decision to complete the form and your answer will not harm you in any way. If you\n                                            want to learn more about the law or this form, visit the U.S. Department of Labor’s Office of Federal Contract Compliance\n                                            Programs (OFCCP) website at www.dol.gov/ofccp . How do you know if you have a disability? A disability is a condition that substantially limits one or more of your “major life activities.” If you have or have ever had\n                                            such a condition, you are a person with a disability. Disabilities include, but are not limited to: Alcohol or other substance use\n                                                disorder (not currently using\n                                                drugs illegally) Autoimmune disorder, for\n                                                example, lupus, fibromyalgia,\n                                                rheumatoid arthritis, HIV/AIDS Blind or low vision Cancer (past or present) Cardiovascular or heart\n                                                disease Celiac disease Cerebral palsy Deaf or serious difficulty\n                                                hearing Diabetes Disfigurement, for example,\n                                                disfigurement caused by burns,\n                                                wounds, accidents, or congenital\n                                                disorders Epilepsy or other seizure disorder Gastrointestinal disorders, for example,\n                                                Crohn's Disease, irritable bowel\n                                                syndrome Intellectual or developmental disability Mental health conditions, for example,\n                                                depression, bipolar disorder, anxiety\n                                                disorder, schizophrenia, PTSD Missing limbs or partially missing limbs Mobility impairment, benefiting from the\n                                                use of a wheelchair, scooter, walker,\n                                                leg brace(s) and/or other supports Nervous system condition, for example,\n                                                migraine headaches, Parkinson’s\n                                                disease, multiple sclerosis (MS) Neurodivergence, for example,\n                                                attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder\n                                                (ADHD), autism spectrum disorder,\n                                                dyslexia, dyspraxia, other learning\n                                                disabilities Partial